WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.760
I've become absolutely obsessed 
with these things.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.214
There are so many of these 
rectangle things everywhere.

00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:09.870
Every new building.

00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:15.436
There, they're subtle.
They're there.

00:00:15.436 --> 00:00:16.746
Right over there.

00:00:16.746 --> 00:00:18.155
They're everywhere.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:21.887
There.

00:00:23.212 --> 00:00:27.116
OK, this isn't a fancy apartment building,
but look at this! Kohl's.

00:00:28.878 --> 00:00:29.926
Look at all those. 

00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:36.532
If you're living in an apartment anywhere
from Minneapolis to Massachusetts

00:00:36.532 --> 00:00:40.372
you'll see a pattern of rectangles 
on the facade just like this.

00:00:41.884 --> 00:00:43.853
Buildings used to look like this.

00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:45.298
Now they look like this.

00:00:46.419 --> 00:00:50.835
But all these rectangles and textures 
are surface evidence 

00:00:50.835 --> 00:00:54.392
of a hidden system that's changed buildings.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.039
Whether it's a big public development, 
like a library or private development

00:01:07.039 --> 00:01:08.395
like apartment building

00:01:08.395 --> 00:01:11.448
you can find panels that
compose the facade.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:18.296
I'm Matt Hogan, and I'm a project architect
with Walter Parks Architects.

00:01:18.296 --> 00:01:21.767
And I'm Walter Parks, and I work at 
Walter Parks Architects with Matt.

00:01:23.499 --> 00:01:30.700
So this building is in an old and historic district, 
and so it has to fit in with the

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.710
historic nature of the other buildings.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:37.290
And if you look at that building and you look
at the building behind you, there's a band

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.548
the building behind you, 
at the top of every window.

00:01:39.548 --> 00:01:41.295
And we have a band at the top 
of every window.

00:01:41.295 --> 00:01:43.720
The running bond pattern mimics the bricks.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.490
We're developing a palette of exterior materials
that complement one another, but also hopefully

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.085
relate in some way to the neighborhood 
that the building is in.

00:01:55.961 --> 00:02:00.932
My name is Andrea Quilici, and I am a senior
associate at Quinn Evans Architects

00:02:00.932 --> 00:02:02.345
and I'm a designer.

00:02:02.345 --> 00:02:05.340
I mostly work on libraries.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.788
There are three projects 
that we design at Quinn Evans.

00:02:09.725 --> 00:02:13.349
So Libbie Mill is located right at the center 
of a new development.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:16.663
We terracotta, we use high performance 
concrete panels

00:02:16.663 --> 00:02:18.534
so that looks like concrete.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:20.624
Varina is a very pastoral.

00:02:20.624 --> 00:02:26.551
It's open, it's next to a beautiful creek 
that it's preserved.

00:02:26.551 --> 00:02:29.920
And then we get to Fairfield this is
much more suburban.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.569
We want selected material that in some way
resonate with this community.

00:02:34.569 --> 00:02:39.064
We want to have a material durable, 
and we use the slate

00:02:39.064 --> 00:02:41.989
because it's a material that
everybody knows.

00:02:41.989 --> 00:02:46.708
The visual choices are different, but these
panels all serve a similar technical purpose.

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:50.797
And they're all only available now because
of the evolution of building design

00:02:50.797 --> 00:02:52.721
during the 20th century.

00:02:52.721 --> 00:02:58.505
A number of trends have driven us towards
actually building car dealership 

00:02:58.505 --> 00:03:04.790
and high rise apartment buildings in the same way 
that we built houses in 1920.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.250
My name is John Straub and I'm a professor
of the University of Waterloo in both the

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.719
Department of Civil Engineering and 
the School of Architecture.

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:24.680
A lot of serious houses that were meant to
be permanent before 1900 would have been built

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.489
out of masonry, out of brick on brick.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:30.772
They would have had plaster 
as an interior finish.

00:03:30.772 --> 00:03:34.442
Brick is great like a big sponge, 
but it's super heavy 

00:03:34.442 --> 00:03:37.072
and requires the expense 
of a mason.

00:03:37.072 --> 00:03:41.564
Those 1920 houses represented a different
approach by using shingles 

00:03:41.564 --> 00:03:43.467
that protected the house from water 

00:03:43.467 --> 00:03:45.879
but weren't necessary
to hold it up.

00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:50.962
By the time we got to the Second World War,
even serious houses

00:03:50.962 --> 00:03:55.335
the houses for the town physician or mayor

00:03:55.345 --> 00:03:59.530
were now being built out of 
wood framing lightweight systems.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:06.480
If I were to go and say, how did we build
a local school or community center in 1930

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.616
or even 1960, the answer would have been masonry.

00:04:11.616 --> 00:04:16.118
But at the same time, even big projects were
slowly shifting 

00:04:16.118 --> 00:04:17.916
to lighter, more flexible systems.

00:04:17.916 --> 00:04:24.576
And as we got into the energy crisis, we even
started to care about their energy efficiency

00:04:24.576 --> 00:04:27.350
of how much it would cost 
to heat and cool them.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.843
I'm the associate director of the 
Pennsylvania Housing Research Center.

00:04:30.843 --> 00:04:35.178
We are a part of Penn State University in
the Civil Environmental Engineering Department.

00:04:35.178 --> 00:04:37.321
Our core vision is rooted in Pennsylvania.

00:04:37.321 --> 00:04:42.599
It's to help that industry build better homes
by making our buildings more energy efficient.

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:47.009
There was a bit of a lag in how 
we controlled moisture.

00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:49.010
That is where the panels come in.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.980
They're part of a system called 
rainscreen cladding.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.914
It takes these high-tech insulated houses

00:04:55.914 --> 00:04:59.615
and adds an air gap between them 
and a rainscreen.

00:04:59.615 --> 00:05:04.448
Often, the fiber cement panels that are so
recognizable, but other textures too.

00:05:04.448 --> 00:05:06.736
It doesn't support the whole structure.

00:05:06.736 --> 00:05:08.470
That's what the frame is for.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:12.783
Imagine hanging a picture 
a half inch out from the wall.

00:05:12.783 --> 00:05:15.620
Panels are simply hung 
on a track system.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:20.529
As this installation video from one popular
manufacturer, Nichiha, shows.

00:05:20.529 --> 00:05:24.680
That rainscreen keeps a lot of water out, 
and what water does get through 

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.964
has time and space to dry in the gap.

00:05:27.964 --> 00:05:33.020
Instead of getting in the structure and rotting
it just like those New England shingles did,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.620
rainscreen cladding keeps water out.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:42.402
But does it also use airflow to create a more
environmentally efficient enclosure?

00:05:42.402 --> 00:05:47.936
Creating the airflow to make it more efficient
is its it's like vast—

00:05:47.936 --> 00:05:49.309
like it's literally nothing.

00:05:49.309 --> 00:05:51.585
It's a bee fart in a windstorm.

00:05:52.672 --> 00:05:56.234
But I would say that there's a couple of factors

00:05:56.234 --> 00:05:59.085
where depending on how you 
look at the environment

00:05:59.085 --> 00:06:04.472
When I said that there's this trend to make,
to use lighter weight less material 

00:06:04.472 --> 00:06:06.658
that is actually environmental.

00:06:06.658 --> 00:06:12.684
I mean, if you were to try and build the buildings
that are needed in the next 30 years 

00:06:12.684 --> 00:06:19.738
around the world and make them out of 
as many tons of material as we built them in 1940

00:06:19.738 --> 00:06:22.261
the planet would just fall apart.

00:06:22.261 --> 00:06:24.460
A rainscreen is nothing more 
than a concept, really.

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:25.959
It's a system.

00:06:25.959 --> 00:06:29.710
It's not any one product, 
it's not any one technique.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:33.740
It's an assembly and that can be applied
to commercial buildings just as much as 

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:36.174
it can be applied to residential buildings.

00:06:36.174 --> 00:06:39.206
This technical adjustment and 
shifting thinking

00:06:39.206 --> 00:06:43.530
led to a big change in what the outside of a building 
could look like.

00:06:45.654 --> 00:06:49.256
I mean, there was a time where 
environmentally conscious building 

00:06:49.256 --> 00:06:51.037
was with sort of on the fringe.

00:06:51.037 --> 00:06:56.515
And now I think because so many of these strategies
have rain screens, for instance, have become

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:59.500
more mainstream, they're seen as best practice.

00:07:00.723 --> 00:07:04.252
Our clients are mostly build and hold guys.

00:07:04.252 --> 00:07:09.300
They're are long-term guys who want to hold
that asset and don't want it falling apart.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.960
They're really worried about 
what it's going to be in 20 years.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:16.005
They've got a 25 year mortgage or
a 30 year mortgage.

00:07:16.752 --> 00:07:18.477
One of they key factors is, for example

00:07:18.477 --> 00:07:19.838
is construction time.

00:07:20.279 --> 00:07:23.699
What's interesting, for example, with this
system is the fact that you can 

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:29.143
enclose the building, dry in the building, 
independently from the facade.

00:07:32.354 --> 00:07:35.944
So certainly availability of labor 
is a big one.

00:07:35.944 --> 00:07:37.330
Cost is a big one as well.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.020
It has structural implications.

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:42.877
Also, when you look at designing the structure
of the home, adding brick will change 

00:07:42.877 --> 00:07:47.524
some of your details or require some additional
additional structural design.

00:07:47.524 --> 00:07:51.246
Easy to overcome, but any of those additional details 
they start to add up.

00:07:51.246 --> 00:07:58.441
You see, if we think about the historic choices
architects had in 1960, pick another date.

00:07:58.441 --> 00:08:00.000
They didn't have a lot.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.927
It was stucco or it was brick.

00:08:02.927 --> 00:08:08.866
And then they had this sudden shock that they
could do things like concrete.

00:08:08.866 --> 00:08:11.280
And they had, like all 4 choices.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:15.898
They were like, their minds were just exploding
about the possibilities.

00:08:15.898 --> 00:08:19.584
That obviously could have completely 
turned on its head today 

00:08:19.584 --> 00:08:23.623
because somewhere from 
the 1960s to the 2020s

00:08:23.623 --> 00:08:27.988
we've gone to having maybe 3 or 4 
dominant cladding types for walls

00:08:27.988 --> 00:08:30.510
to having easy 40 or 50.

